# What to ask for. One application per line.
#
# Everything after `::` is passed to the application when it runs. `{in}` is
# whichever folder it asked to read, as the application sees it from inside its
# container — the harness fills that in from what the run actually mounted,
# because the name depends on the path and is not worth predicting.
#
# A line without `::` is still generated and still started; it just runs with no
# arguments, which for most of these prints their usage.
#
# The list is deliberately varied. Six of these want to read a file, one wants
# to install a package and write one, and one needs nothing at all — a model
# that only ever produces "read a CSV, print a table" would look perfect here if
# the list were all CSVs.

compare two CSV files and show me what changed :: {in}/before.csv {in}/after.csv
count how many times each word appears in a text file :: {in}/notes.txt
find the error lines in a log file and group them by hour :: {in}/app.log
print a table of contents for a Markdown file :: {in}/handbook.md
find the rows of a CSV that have a missing value, and say which column :: {in}/messy.csv
turn a folder of JSON files into one CSV :: {in}/json
show me how long the lines in a text file are, as a histogram :: {in}/notes.txt
make a QR code image for a URL :: https://github.com/JGalego/Ephemeral
